` `for-below` asks for the type on behalf of a class below that declares the property again.
` A value that is being typed has no type to give such a class, and is not a circle: the
` class below takes its own value's type. A stored property's value is evaluated by the
` constructor of each class that holds it, and is typed for that class. A lazy property of a
` class is evaluated by one function, which the classes below share: its value is typed
` once, for the class that declares it, as the reference types it. So is a state machine a
` property of a class holds: the classes below that declare it no value of their own start
` the one typed for the class that declares it, and share its functions, and those that refine
` it share it as a piece of theirs (`MachineComposite`). A class that declares a property with
` no value and gives it one in a root `<This` writes its type in one and its value in the
` other. One a line of a class's markup declares has the type of the value the line gives it
` (`content-property-type`), and one that projects a case of a machine of the markup the
` optional of what the case holds (`projection-type`). A state machine that refines one a class
` above declares, read while that one is typed, has the type it has from above and is typed
` later (`refines-in-progress`).
